WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:13.849
Oh! there, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:16.050
I didn't see you for a second.

3
00:00:16.911 --> 00:00:19.093
I guess I was too busy working on my design.

4
00:00:20.054 --> 00:00:22.796
I'm going to enter it in a contest at the community playground.

5
00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:28.060
First prize for designing the best playground fort is one-hundred dollars!

6
00:00:29.241 --> 00:00:35.218
And speaking of forts, this week in our landmarks lesson, we learned about a really famous one: the

7
00:00:35.278 --> 00:00:36.959
Kremlin in Moscow, Russia.

8
00:00:37.999 --> 00:00:43.462
Lots of Russian cities have a kremlin—the Russian word for fortress or fort—but the one in Moscow is

9
00:00:43.562 --> 00:00:46.424
so famous that most people just call it the Kremlin.

10
00:00:47.404 --> 00:00:51.907
The Kremlin is an enormous collection of buildings that's shaped kind of like a triangle.

11
00:00:52.888 --> 00:01:00.132
And with its amazing cathedrals, palaces, and tall, colorful towers, the Kremlin is often described as an "architectural

12
00:01:00.232 --> 00:01:07.460
wonder." When it was first built back in 1156, the Kremlin was just a small wooden fortress.

13
00:01:08.661 --> 00:01:13.084
Over time more buildings were added to the site, especially during the fifteenth century.

14
00:01:14.185 --> 00:01:18.528
That was when a czar named Ivan the Great ruled Russia, and he wanted to show off his

15
00:01:18.588 --> 00:01:20.109
power by expanding the place.

16
00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:26.153
Later other Russian rulers rebuilt some of the structures and added even more buildings to the grounds.

17
00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:33.097
Since this expansion occurred over a few centuries, there's now a huge variety of architectural styles at the

18
00:01:33.137 --> 00:01:39.559
Kremlin. The entire complex is surrounded by thick brick walls that have twenty towers built into them.

19
00:01:40.639 --> 00:01:42.720
Each tower is unique with its own name.

20
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.081
The oldest of these is the Secret Tower, named this because it used to contain a secret tunnel

21
00:01:49.121 --> 00:01:50.522
that led to the Moskva River.

22
00:01:51.582 --> 00:01:56.864
So even if the Kremlin had been completely surrounded by an enemy army, people could have still sneaked

23
00:01:56.904 --> 00:01:58.004
to the river to get water!

24
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:06.544
At the very center of the Kremlin is Cathedral Square, a large open space surrounded by—you guessed it—cathedrals!

25
00:02:07.745 --> 00:02:12.387
In fact, many people think these buildings are among the most beautiful churches in the world.

26
00:02:12.787 --> 00:02:16.249
The Cathedral of the Assumption is the oldest and largest of these.

27
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:21.752
Unfortunately, when the cathedral was almost complete, it was destroyed by an earthquake.

28
00:02:22.813 --> 00:02:26.975
However, work on the cathedral was continued, and in 1479 it was finally finished.

29
00:02:30.092 --> 00:02:35.315
The Cathedral of the Assumption is a massive building topped with five golden onion domes.

30
00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:40.158
The domes are called this because they look like well, onions, of course!

31
00:02:41.238 --> 00:02:41.978
According to Mr.

32
00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:45.340
Fay, onion domes are very common in Russian architecture.

33
00:02:46.341 --> 00:02:51.764
Another cathedral at the square, the Cathedral of the Annunciation, was used as a private chapel for the

34
00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:57.967
czars. Except there was one czar who wasn't allowed to enter it—Ivan the Terrible.

35
00:02:58.908 --> 00:03:04.483
Back then, according to the Russian Orthodox Church, a person could be married only three times.

36
00:03:05.604 --> 00:03:11.008
Well, Ivan the Terrible got married four times, so he was no longer permitted inside the church.

37
00:03:12.729 --> 00:03:16.952
Guess what Ivan the Terrible did to solve the problem He added a porch to the building!

38
00:03:17.992 --> 00:03:22.095
That way he could sit outside and still hear any religious services going on inside.

39
00:03:23.076 --> 00:03:28.619
Right across from the Cathedral of the Annunciation is the Ivan the Great Bell Tower, the tallest building

40
00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:29.260
in the Kremlin.

41
00:03:30.228 --> 00:03:34.532
The Czar Bell, the largest bell in the world, sits at the foot of this tower.

42
00:03:35.593 --> 00:03:42.318
Mr. Fay told us it weighs just over 181 metric tons, which made me wonder something: what does

43
00:03:42.398 --> 00:03:43.999
a bell that big sound like?

44
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.583
Well, nobody knows because the Czar Bell has never been rung.

45
00:03:49.584 --> 00:03:53.607
It got too hot while it was being cast, and a big piece broke off.

46
00:03:55.309 --> 00:03:59.292
Today about two-thirds of the Kremlin is used for official government business.

47
00:04:00.392 --> 00:04:03.695
In fact, the Senate Building is the official home of the Russian president.

48
00:04:04.676 --> 00:04:07.638
But there are many buildings that are open to the public for touring.

49
00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:12.542
A popular spot is the Armory, which is one of the biggest museums in the world.

50
00:04:13.583 --> 00:04:22.651
It holds lots of priceless artifacts collected by Russian czars over the centuries—swords, armor, pistols, clothing, jewelry, carriages,

51
00:04:23.071 --> 00:04:24.732
even Ivan the Terrible's throne!

52
00:04:25.813 --> 00:04:30.869
Visitors to the Armory can also check out the State Diamond Fund, a collection of Russian gems that

53
00:04:30.909 --> 00:04:33.089
includes the largest sapphire in the world.

54
00:04:34.209 --> 00:04:37.390
The State Kremlin Palace is another building that's open to the public.

55
00:04:38.151 --> 00:04:42.072
Built in the 1960s, this building is the newest one inside the complex.

56
00:04:43.772 --> 00:04:44.713
In fact, Mr.

57
00:04:44.753 --> 00:04:49.814
Fay said that its plain, straight lines and tall, modern windows make it look out of place among

58
00:04:49.854 --> 00:04:50.714
the older buildings.

59
00:04:51.935 --> 00:04:56.376
It has a huge 6,000-seat auditorium, which is used today as a concert hall.

60
00:04:57.428 --> 00:05:02.132
If you ever go to the Kremlin, you'll also see another important world landmark: Red Square.

61
00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:08.858
This large public space sits just outside the Kremlin's entrance and it was once the site of battles

62
00:05:08.918 --> 00:05:10.099
and military parades.

63
00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:15.083
But now it's used mainly for things like rock concerts and fashion shows.

64
00:05:16.284 --> 00:05:18.326
I bet it's a cool place to see a concert!

65
00:05:19.447 --> 00:05:21.008
At one end of the square is St.

66
00:05:21.068 --> 00:05:25.432
Basil's Cathedral, which Ivan the Terrible commissioned to celebrate a military victory.

67
00:05:26.388 --> 00:05:29.391
This cathedral is known for its brightly colored onion domes.

68
00:05:31.192 --> 00:05:36.356
Each dome has a unique color pattern and texture, giving the cathedral an extremely distinct appearance.

69
00:05:37.517 --> 00:05:43.422
Supposedly Ivan the Terrible wanted to make sure that the architect who designed the cathedral never built anything

70
00:05:43.462 --> 00:05:44.563
more beautiful than St.

71
00:05:44.583 --> 00:05:49.507
Basil's. So when the cathedral was finished, Ivan had him blinded!

72
00:05:50.587 --> 00:05:52.889
Or so says a popular legend—Mr.

73
00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:56.112
Fay told us historians are sure the story isn't true.

74
00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:02.043
But just in case, I'm glad Ivan the Terrible isn't judging the contest for best playground fort!

75
00:06:03.104 --> 00:06:06.906
That reminds me I have to get back to my design for the new playground fort.

76
00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:09.308
Wish me luck in the contest!
